---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import SurvivalDiagram from '../../components/SurvivalDiagram.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import release from '../../data/release.json';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Why Residiuum: independent survival"
  description="How Residiuum reports damage as explicit holes, preserves independently verifiable data islands, and recovers surviving material without catalogs."
  path="/survival/"
  eyebrow="Why Residiuum"
  lead="Ordinary databases ask whether the whole machine can be reconstructed. Residiuum first asks which pieces can still be proved intact."
>
  <h2>The ordinary failure model</h2>
  <p>
    Most databases behave as a single logical machine. When critical metadata,
    file structure, or engine state is damaged, healthy bytes may still exist
    but become inaccessible. Recovery commonly means restoring an intact
    replica, accepting a valid prefix, or reconstructing the database as a
    whole.
  </p>
  <p>
    That model is all-or-nothing at the wrong layer: local corruption can make
    unrelated healthy material unreachable.
  </p>

  <h2>Independent survival units</h2>
  <Claim id="claim.damage.independent-islands" showBadge={true} />
  <p>
    Residiuum treats a store as a fabric of independently survivable data
    islands—not one all-or-nothing object. Each unit carries enough structure
    to verify itself. Destruction of one unit does not silently poison its
    neighbors.
  </p>

  <div class="diagram-block">
    <SurvivalDiagram />
  </div>

  <h2>Verification and explicit holes</h2>
  <Claim id="claim.damage.explicit-holes" showBadge={true} />
  <p>
    Missing or unreadable material is reported as a <strong>hole</strong>. Holes
    are part of the recovery report. They are not allowed to disappear, and they
    are not allowed to invalidate unrelated verified material.
  </p>
  <Callout title="What damage tolerance does not do" variant="warning">
    <p>
      Damage tolerance reduces blast radius. It does <strong>not</strong> make
      missing or overwritten bytes recoverable. If the only copy of a payload
      is gone, Residiuum reports the hole—it does not invent content.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Authoritative versus derived state</h2>
  <Claim id="claim.indexes.derived-accelerators" showBadge={true} />
  <p>
    Authoritative records live in self-verifying storage units. Catalogs and
    indexes are derived accelerators: rebuildable maps that speed access. If
    they are lost or stale, surviving segments remain the source of truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Catalog-independent salvage</h2>
  <Claim id="claim.salvage.catalog-independent" showBadge={true} />
  <p>
    Salvage walks surviving frames and produces evidence of what verified,
    what was partial, and what is missing—without depending on catalogs or
    secondary indexes as the only map back to the data.
  </p>

  <h2>What cannot be recovered</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bytes that were never written or were overwritten</li>
    <li>Material destroyed on every remaining copy</li>
    <li>Secrets that only lived in volatile memory</li>
    <li>Application meaning that was never recorded in the envelope</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Replication, backup, and salvage</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Recovery modes" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Mode</th>
          <th scope="col">What it is for</th>
          <th scope="col">What it is not</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Replication</td>
          <td>Keep additional live copies under a cluster/replica policy</td>
          <td>A substitute for salvage when every copy is damaged the same way</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Backup</td>
          <td>Intentional package of a store for restore</td>
          <td>Damage recovery of a corrupted live root</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Salvage</td>
          <td>Evidence recovery of verified frames from damaged media</td>
          <td>A full logical export of only live latest values</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Demonstration</h2>
  <p>
    When the repository documents a stable operator journey for “damage then
    examine,” that walkthrough lives on the documentation site. Start with the
    recovery guide and the capability matrix for current salvage and scrub
    surfaces.
  </p>
  <div class="btn-row">
    <ExternalLink href={release.docsRecovery} class="btn btn-primary">
      Read recovery guide
    </ExternalLink>
    <a href="/status/" class="btn btn-secondary">Status and limitations</a>
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .diagram-block {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
</style>
